@model EmployeeListItemViewModel
@*
    What this unit is to this person: where they mainly work, somewhere they are also counted, or
    a unit they head without being a member of. A partial with a switch expression, like
    _EmploymentStatusName, for the same two reasons: the names stay literals the localiser and
    LocalisationResourceTests can both see, and a switch statement in the middle of markup reads to
    UserVisibleStringTests as rendered text.

    "Main" and "Additional", not "Primary" and "Secondary". Secondary reads as a judgement about
    the person — a junior posting — when it is a statement about the unit's share of them. The
    internal names are unchanged; this is the word a reader sees.

    "Head (not a member)" is ADR-0012's case said plainly. Without it a head borrowed from another
    department reads as somebody who works here, and the headcount beside them — which does not
    count them — looks wrong instead of right.

    There is no "home: …" line under an Additional row, and there must not be one.

    It was here, and it repeated the unit the row already names. The placement column beside it
    shows the *main* placement, resolved on the same structure this list is filtered to — which is
    the home department, by definition — so "home: Civil" sat next to a cell already reading
    "Civil" on every Additional row the demo tenant has. Ahmed Khan's row on Site Team A read
    "Civil · Additional · 40% · home: Civil". Where the person's other placements are is now one
    question answered in one place: the "+N more" disclosure in the placement column, with the full
    path and the share of each.
*@
@(Model.RoleHere switch
{
    UnitRole.Primary => T["Main"],
    UnitRole.Secondary => T["Additional"],
    UnitRole.HeadOnly => T["Head (not a member)"],

    // Null on the unfiltered list, where there is no unit for a placement to be relative to.
    _ => null,
})
